# Publishing filters BOTH the source upload and what the build stage hands to
# the run stage. That second half is the trap: excluding a build output here
# does not mean "rebuild it in the deployment", it means the run stage never
# sees it. Excluding `backend/target/` and `frontend/dist/` produced a build
# that succeeded and then
#   fork/exec backend/target/release/backend: no such file or directory
#
# So only things the running app genuinely never reads belong here.
# `backend/target/` still does — it reaches several GB — but the build now
# copies the one file that matters out of it into `bin/` (see .replit), and
# `bin/` is deliberately not listed. `frontend/dist/` must ship: it IS
# STATIC_DIR.
backend/target/
frontend/node_modules/

# Never publish a local database or a stale snapshot artefact. The deployment
# restores its own from App Storage; a copy shipped in the tree would be
# picked up by restore_if_absent's "already present" branch and silently win
# over the real one.
*.db
*.db-shm
*.db-wal
*.db.snapshot
*.db.restore
# A seed snapshot dragged into the workspace to be uploaded to App Storage.
# It belongs in the bucket, not in the publish snapshot — shipping it would
# add ~19 MB to every deploy for a file the app never reads from disk.
*.db.gz
*.sqlite.gz
